  "assumptions": [
    "This is an illustration: a made-up loss bowl, loss = (3x² + z²)/2, with simulated batch noise. No real model or data is used.",
    "Each step: new position = old − learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has standard deviation 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. That gives 1.5 at b = 1 and exactly 0 at b = 64. The noise comes from a fixed seed, the run number and the step number.",
    "Both arrows show steps: the orange one is the next mini-batch step and the grey one is learning rate × the full-data downhill slope. Both are drawn at 2× their true length in bowl units.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged if the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| > 2). In the steep direction, steps grow without noise once the learning rate is above 2/3.",
    "The bowl is drawn at 0.5 × loss. A diverged ball and path are held at the edge of the bowl, but the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail shows the start plus up to 40 landing points. The ghost keeps the last run's path. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average."
  ]
}